How to Stop Using DynamoDB for Terraform State Locking (in later version of TF. Here’s Why)

You don’t need to anymore.
With the latest Terraform versions, S3 alone can now lock and unlock state files using object locking and versioning.

Do this in Terragrunt: 

remote_state {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket         = "my-tf-state"
    key            = "${path_relative_to_include()}/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "eu-west-2"
    encrypt        = true
    bucket         = "my-locked-tf-bucket"
    object_locking = true
  }
}

📌 Steps to make it work:
	1.	Enable Object Locking when creating the S3 bucket (must be done up front).
	2.	Turn on versioning.
	3.	Use latest Terraform + AWS provider versions.

🚫 No more extra infra (bye DynamoDB).
🔐 Locking is handled directly at the S3 level.

Simple. Secure. Modern.
